{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Meeting New People"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Let's Talk!"}, {"type": "position", "content": 4}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "Beginner"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Adding an -s to nouns"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "A noun is a person, place or thing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Add an <span class=\"strong\">-s</span> to make the noun plural:   <br /><br />  one apple (singular noun),  two apple<span class=\"strong\">s (plural noun) </span>   <br />  one girlfriend (singular noun),  two girlfriend<span class=\"strong\">s (plural noun) </span>  <br />  one nationality (singular noun), two nationalitie<span class=\"strong\">s (plural noun) </span>  <br /><br /> The <span class=\"strong\">-s</span> sounds like <span class=\"strong\">/z/</span>.<span class=\"italic\"> <br />Example: girlfriendz, nationalitiez</span> <br /><br />Words that end in -ch, x, s or needs an -es for the plural:  <br /><span class=\"italic\"> <br />Example:</span> <br />more than one match = matches <br />more than one fox = foxes <br />more than one kiss = kisses <br />"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":\"03\",\"columns\":\"02\",\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Nouns </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Examples</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Singular noun (1 noun)</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Joe's <span class=\\\"strong\\\">girlfriend</span> is from Canada. </span> <br />Her<span class=\\\"strong\\\"> nationality</span> is Colombian. </span>\\\"}\